Why: a decompilation that runs too long has to be stopped from inside the
decompiler. Ghidra's Java side stops one by killing the decompiler process
when its timeout (30 seconds by default) passes; a WebAssembly module runs
on the one thread that called it, with no process to kill and no other
thread to interrupt it, so the decompiler itself has to look at the time.
This adds Action::checkpoint, a hook called before each attempt to apply an
Action (every pass of every action and rule pool), null by default. The host
layer sets it to a function that throws a LowlevelError once the call's
deadline has passed (host/src/program.cc), which unwinds the decompilation
like any other LowlevelError. Unset, as in Ghidra's own console, nothing
changes. It cannot be done in host/: the action tree is built and run
inside the library, and no virtual function is called often enough in every
phase of it.
Upstream: not reported; upstream has no use for it, since its Java side
kills the process.

--- a/cpp/action.hh
+++ b/cpp/action.hh
@@ -100,6 +100,11 @@
 #endif
   virtual void printStatistics(ostream &s) const;		///< Dump statistics to stream
   int4 perform(Funcdata &data); 				///< Perform this action (if necessary)
+  /// \brief A function called before each attempt to apply an Action, or null
+  ///
+  /// A host may set it to stop a decompilation that runs too long, by throwing a LowlevelError
+  /// once its deadline has passed. (decompiler-js patch, not upstream)
+  static void (*checkpoint)(Funcdata &data);
   bool setBreakPoint(uint4 tp,const string &specify);		///< Set a breakpoint on this action
   virtual void clearBreakPoints(void);				///< Clear all breakpoints set on \b this Action
   bool setWarning(bool val,const string &specify);		///< Set a warning on this action
--- a/cpp/action.cc
